---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../components/EmailCapture.astro';
// Destinations come from the registry, never hardcoded. Move an article or
// rename a dictionary anchor and every link on the site follows, and
// check-concepts.mjs fails the build if a destination stops resolving.
import { hrefFor } from '../data/concepts.js';

// THE PREVENTION HUB, rebuilt 2026-07-29 as a professional doctrine (Antonio's
// spec, approved in full). What changed and why:
//
// - "The mandatory rules" became **THE PROFESSIONAL RULES**. He ruled the
//   title stays EVERGREEN with NO COUNT in it (his own lint rule from
//   2026-07-27 stands: a counted promise rots the day rule eleven arrives).
//   The 01-10 numbering on the cards carries the "ten rules" feeling instead,
//   because numbers inside a list are allowed; numbers in a PROMISE are not.
// - Two bands, YOUR MUSIC (01-06) and YOUR GIG (07-10). Ten flat cards read
//   as a wall; the bands give the page rhythm and make it scannable.
//   NIGHT -> GIG, 2026-08-03 (Antonio): "let's not put your night, because
//   this could be sunset, morning, you never know." He is right, and it was
//   not one label: this page used "night" as a synonym for "gig" in SIX
//   places. The audience rule here is PROFESSIONALS ONLY, and professionals
//   play festival afternoons, sunset beach sets, brunch parties and
//   after-hours that end at eleven in the morning. "Night" quietly told a
//   large part of that audience the page was not written for them.
//   GIG also beats "set" (too narrow, 07-10 include what you do BEFORE you
//   play) and "gig day" (breaks the Your X parallel and collides with rule
//   07, titled "Gig day is a freeze"), and it echoes the brand name.
//   DO NOT reintroduce "night" as a stand-in for the gig anywhere on the site.
// - Cards stay EXPANDED. No accordions here, deliberately: each rule is two
//   short lines, so the collapse doctrine does not bite, and the whole point
//   is that a DJ can scan the doctrine in one pass.
// - Rule 10 is CONTINUOUS LEARNING (his call, replacing an earlier draft that
//   pointed at the four rescue moves). It connects Prevention to the
//   Knowledge Base and the FAQ, and it carries the page's closing thought:
//   professionals prepare their minds, not just their gear.
// - The old "gig day is a freeze" alert card moved INSIDE the ten as rule 07.
// - Emoji removed from the page (banned in headings sitewide).
// - Title and description broadened off "USB prevention": this is the
//   Prevention Hub, not USB Prevention.
//
// FACT DISCIPLINE: every claim here already shipped verified elsewhere on the
// site (facts.js, /knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox, the /fix library) or is habit advice
// that needs no source. The rekordbox backup path is the verified one from the
// 7.2.3 manual: File > Library > Backup Library.

const MUSIC = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'Two identical drives, every gig.',
    // The card belongs in the gig-bag rule and nowhere else on this page: it
    // is the third thing that goes in the bag, after the two drives. `plain()`
    // strips the tag for the ItemList, so the schema still reads as the words
    // on screen.
    body: 'The same export on both, made in the same session from the same playlists. One in the bag, one in your pocket, and <a href="/card">the printable Emergency Card</a> beside them.',
    why: 'One drive is convenience. Two drives are insurance.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Format for the whole room.',
    // FAT32 and MBR ARE LINKED (Antonio, 2026-08-03): "if the person doesn't
    // know what this is, you have to send it to the dictionary, otherwise the
    // person will be stuck there. What is MBR? What is FAT32?" This rule gave
    // an instruction in two acronyms and left a reader who did not already
    // know them with nowhere to go.
    // They resolve to ARTICLES, not the dictionary, because the registry rule
    // is that a fuller article wins when one exists. hrefFor() decides, so
    // this page never has to know which.
    // ONLY TWO OF THE FOUR TERMS ARE LINKED. exFAT and NTFS are named here
    // only to say do not use them, and the sentence already explains itself
    // ("read only by newer units", "by none of them"), so a reader needs no
    // detour to act. Four links in two sentences would be its own problem;
    // the standing steer is that linking stays editorial, not automatic.
    body: `<a href="${hrefFor('fat32')}">FAT32</a> with an <a href="${hrefFor('mbr')}"><span class="mono" translate="no">MBR</span></a> partition scheme is read by every rekordbox-era Pioneer DJ and AlphaTheta player, old and new. exFAT is read only by newer units, and NTFS by none of them.`,
    why: 'You are not formatting for your player. You are formatting for a booth you have never seen.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Buy drives that earn their place.',
    body: 'A no-name stick is where most of these stories begin. Carry drives with a reputation among working DJs. <a href="/gear">The drives worth carrying</a>.',
    why: 'The cheapest part of your setup carries the only thing the venue cannot replace.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Export fresh, every time.',
    body: 'Re-export the playlists you are about to play after any library change, and always <strong>export</strong> from rekordbox. Never drag audio files onto the drive by hand.',
    why: 'An old export is a corrupt export waiting to happen. A dragged folder has no database at all.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'Eject like it matters.',
    body: 'Eject from rekordbox and wait for the computer to release the drive. On a player, press <span class="mono" translate="no">USB STOP</span> and let the light settle before the drive moves.',
    why: 'Interrupting a write is the fastest way to corrupt the database you just spent an hour building.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    title: 'Back up the library, not just the drive.',
    body: 'In rekordbox: <span class="mono" translate="no">File &gt; Library &gt; Backup Library</span>, monthly, onto a different drive or the cloud. Your cues, grids and playlists live in the library, not on the stick.',
    why: 'A USB is a working copy. If it is your only copy, you do not have a backup.',
  },
];

const GIG = [
  {
    n: '07',
    title: 'Gig day is a freeze.',
    body: 'No rekordbox update, no player firmware, no operating system update on the day you play or the days around it. Update the week after a gig, never the day before one.',
    why: 'A new version can bring a new problem, and the booth is where you would meet it. Player firmware has been withdrawn by its own manufacturer after playlists stopped appearing for DJs.',
  },
  {
    n: '08',
    title: 'Test it before you leave.',
    body: 'Load a track from the drive on a player if you own one. If you do not, reopen the export in rekordbox and confirm the device and the playlists are really there.',
    why: 'Ten seconds at home costs nothing. The same discovery in the booth costs the set.',
  },
  {
    n: '09',
    title: 'Know the booth before you walk in.',
    body: 'Ask the promoter which players and mixer are installed, and whether they are linked. Then pack for that room: the export a CDJ-3000X reads is not the one a CDJ-2000NXS2 reads. <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox#which-player">Which player reads which</a>.',
    why: 'Compatibility is a question you answer at home, with time, or in the booth, without it.',
  },
  {
    n: '10',
    title: 'Learn before you need it.',
    body: 'Read one thing while nothing is wrong: an error code, a compatibility quirk, a rescue move. <a href="/knowledge">The knowledge base</a> and <a href="/faq">the FAQ</a> are built for the quiet afternoon, not only for the emergency.',
    why: 'Nobody learns under pressure. They remember. Familiarity is what turns a disaster into an inconvenience.',
  },
];

// THE RULES, MADE MACHINE-READABLE (2026-08-04). This page is the site's
// answer to "how do I stop this happening again", and none of it was in any
// markup, so an answer engine asked that question had nothing here to cite.
//
// ItemList and not HowTo: these are standing habits, not steps of a single
// procedure with a start and an end, and marking a permanent rule up as a
// step would misrepresent what the page says. The order is the page's own
// (the order the gig actually happens), so the list declares itself ordered.
//
// Every name and description is the rule's visible title and its visible
// body, from the same arrays the page renders from. `body` is written with
// inline links and printed with set:html, so tags are stripped here to leave
// exactly the words on screen, nothing added and nothing invented.
// Strips the markup AND decodes the handful of entities the bodies use, so
// the string in the JSON equals the string on screen. Without the decode the
// markup would ship a literal "File &gt; Library" that no page contains.
const plain = (s) => s
  .replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '')
  .replace(/&gt;/g, '>')
  .replace(/&lt;/g, '<')
  .replace(/&nbsp;/g, ' ')
  .replace(/&amp;/g, '&')
  .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
  .trim();
const rulesSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ItemList',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/prepare#rules',
  name: 'The professional rules',
  description: 'The habits that stop DJ gig failures before they reach the booth, in the order the gig actually happens: the ones that protect your music, then the ones that protect your gig.',
  numberOfItems: MUSIC.length + GIG.length,
  itemListOrder: 'https://schema.org/ItemListOrderAscending',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/prepare#webpage' },
  inLanguage: 'en',
  itemListElement: [...MUSIC, ...GIG].map((r, i) => ({
    '@type': 'ListItem',
    position: i + 1,
    name: plain(r.title),
    description: plain(r.body),
  })),
};
---
<Base
  title="The Professional Rules: Prevent DJ Gig Failures | Save My Gig"
  description="The habits that make gig failure a non-event: two drives, clean exports, proper ejects, no updates on gig day, and knowing the booth before you go."
  path="/prepare"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(rulesSchema)} />
  <main class="wrap">
    <div class="status-line amber">Prevention_Hub</div>

    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1rem">NEVER <span class="accent">AGAIN</span></h1>
    <p class="dim pv-lead">
      Most DJ disasters are not bad luck. They are a missing system.
      Professionals do not rely on memory or on the gig going well, they rely
      on preparation. These are the habits that stop problems before they ever
      reach the booth.
    </p>

    <h2 class="pv-h">The <span class="accent">professional</span> rules</h2>
    <p class="dim pv-sub">Non-negotiable, in the order the gig actually happens.</p>

    <div class="pv-band">Your music</div>
    {MUSIC.map((r) => (
      <details class="acc-sec pv-rule" name="pv-acc">
        <summary>
          <span class="pv-head">
            <span class="pv-n" aria-hidden="true">{r.n}</span>
            <h3 class="acc-h2 pv-title">{r.title}</h3>
          </span>
          <span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="acc-body pv-in">
          <p class="pv-text" set:html={r.body} />
          <p class="pv-why"><span>Why it matters</span>{r.why}</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    ))}

    <div class="pv-band">Your gig</div>
    {GIG.map((r) => (
      <details class="acc-sec pv-rule" name="pv-acc">
        <summary>
          <span class="pv-head">
            <span class="pv-n" aria-hidden="true">{r.n}</span>
            <h3 class="acc-h2 pv-title">{r.title}</h3>
          </span>
          <span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="acc-body pv-in">
          <p class="pv-text" set:html={r.body} />
          <p class="pv-why"><span>Why it matters</span>{r.why}</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    ))}

    <p class="pv-close">
      Professionals do not just prepare their gear. They prepare their minds.
    </p>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.6rem">Before you leave: the checklist</h2>
      <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:1rem">This is where the rules become a routine. Tick off the music, the technical gear and the logistics you forget on the way out. It tracks your readiness and saves on this device for next time.</p>
      <a href="/checklist" class="btn btn-go" data-event="checklist_open" data-placement="prepare">Open the pre-gig checklist</a>
    </div>

    <EmailCapture
      source="prepare"
      title="The Emergency Card"
      blurb="One page. Everything to check before you leave, and the first moves when a player refuses your drive. Read it free any time, or get the printable version emailed so it lives on your phone."
    />

    <div class="grid grid-2 section">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The gear that does not die</h3>
        <p>The exact drives worth carrying, and why DJs trust them.</p>
        <a href="/gear" class="btn btn-neutral" data-event="affiliate_click" data-program="gear_page" data-placement="prepare">See the kit</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Master your tools</h3>
        <p>rekordbox, the players and the mixers in depth. Rule 10, in practice.</p>
        <a href="/knowledge" class="btn btn-neutral" data-event="step_completed" data-step="knowledge_click" data-placement="prepare">Open the knowledge base</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .pv-lead { max-width: 44rem; line-height: 1.65; margin-bottom: 2.6rem; font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .pv-h {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 2.05rem); text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em; border: none; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .pv-sub { font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  /* Band headers are the index for the two halves of the doctrine: the rules
     that protect your music, then the rules that protect your gig. */
  .pv-band {
    font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-faint);
    border-bottom: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
    margin: 2.6rem 0 1.1rem;
  }

  /* ACCORDIONS, not open cards (Antonio, after seeing the expanded version
     live: ten expanded rules is "a huge list", and on a phone the page ran
     past 6000px). This is the collapse doctrine applied to the doctrine page
     itself: number and title are the index, the reasoning is one tap away.
     Shared .acc-sec vocabulary from global.css, so it behaves exactly like
     the FAQ, rekordbox and hardware pages. name="pv-acc" makes them
     exclusive: click, read, click the next, the page never grows. */
  .pv-rule { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pv-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9rem; min-width: 0; }
  .pv-n {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.3rem;
    line-height: 1; color: var(--red); letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .pv-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.22; letter-spacing: -0.015em; text-transform: none;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  /* The body lines up under the title, not under the number. */
  .pv-in { padding-left: calc(1.25rem + 1.3rem + 0.9rem); }
  .pv-text { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.94rem; line-height: 1.62; margin: 0; }
  .pv-text strong { color: var(--text); }
  .pv-why {
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0; padding-top: 0.7rem;
    border-top: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-soft);
    color: var(--text-faint); font-size: 0.87rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .pv-why span {
    display: block; font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim); margin-bottom: 0.22rem;
  }

  .pv-close {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 4vw, 1.5rem); line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em; color: var(--text);
    max-width: 30rem; margin: 2.8rem 0 2.4rem;
  }

  /* At 360px the numeral cannot also cost an indent: the body goes full width
     and the gap tightens so titles keep to two lines. */
  @media (max-width: 430px) {
    .pv-head { gap: 0.65rem; }
    .pv-n { font-size: 1.15rem; }
    .pv-in { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  }
</style>
